<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>environmental-issues</title></head><body><h1>environmental-issues</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Air, water, and solid waste pollution</li></ul><h2>Air, water, and solid waste pollution</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Air Pollution</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>- **Primary pollutants**: Directly emitted (SO2, NOx, CO, particulates)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>- **Secondary pollutants**: Formed in atmosphere (ozone, acid rain)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Sources**: Vehicles, industries, burning fossil fuels, volcanic eruptions</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **Effects**: Respiratory diseases, acid rain, smog, global warming</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Control**: Catalytic converters, scrubbers, renewable energy, afforestation</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>### 2. Water Pollution</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- **Point source**: Single identifiable source (factory pipe)</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Non-point source**: Diffuse runoff (agricultural chemicals)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Organic waste**: Sewage, food processing - increases BOD</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Eutrophication**: Excess nutrients -> algal bloom -> oxygen depletion</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- **Heavy metals**: Mercury (Minamata disease), Lead (neurotoxic)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>- **Control**: Wastewater treatment, reduce chemical use, maintain riparian zones</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>### 3. Solid Waste Management</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>- **Biodegradable**: Food waste, paper, garden waste - compostable</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>- **Non-biodegradable**: Plastic, glass, metal - recycling needed</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>- **Hazardous**: Medical waste, batteries, electronics - special disposal</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>- **Methods**: Landfilling, incineration, composting, recycling, 3Rs (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle)</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>- **E-waste**: Rapidly growing problem with toxic components</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>### 4. Acid Rain</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>- Caused by SO2 and NOx reacting with water vapor</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>- Forms sulfuric and nitric acid in rain</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>- Damages buildings, aquatic ecosystems, forests, soil</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>- pH less than 5.6 is considered acid rain</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>### 5. Greenhouse Effect and Global Warming</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>- **Greenhouse gases**: CO2, CH4, N2O, CFCs, water vapor</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>- **Enhanced greenhouse effect**: Human activities increase GHG concentrations</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>- **Effects**: Rising temperatures, sea level rise, extreme weather, melting glaciers</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>- **Kyoto Protocol (1997)**: International agreement to reduce GHG emissions</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>- **Paris Agreement (2015)**: Limit warming to well below 2°C above pre-industrial</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>### 6. Ozone Layer Depletion</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>- **Ozone (O3)**: Protects Earth from harmful UV radiation</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>- **CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons)**: Release chlorine atoms that destroy ozone</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>- **Ozone hole**: Over Antarctica, discovered in 1985</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>- **Montreal Protocol (1987)**: Phased out CFCs successfully</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>- **Effects of depletion**: Skin cancer, cataracts, immune suppression, crop damage</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>### 7. Entrance Exam Focus</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>Causes and effects of pollution types; Greenhouse gases; Ozone depletion mechanism.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Sources of air pollution are vehicles, industries, the burning of fossil fuels, brick kilns and volcanic eruptions.</td></tr><tr><td>Control of air pollution uses catalytic converters (CO to CO2 and NOx to N2), scrubbers that remove SO2, low-sulphur fuels, afforestation and renewable energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Eutrophication sequence: nutrient enrichment leads to an algal bloom, which blocks light, and the dead algae are decomposed by bacteria, raising BOD and lowering dissolved oxygen until fish die.</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy-metal pollutants are mercury, which caused Minamata disease, lead, which is neurotoxic, and cadmium, which damages the kidney; all are bioaccumulated along the food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>The 3Rs, Reduce, Reuse and Recycle, are preferred to landfilling, and composting of biodegradable waste returns nutrients to the soil.</td></tr><tr><td>E-waste contains lead, mercury and cadmium, so it must be collected and treated separately from ordinary municipal solid waste.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>x Ozone is always bad. x Stratospheric ozone protects us; ground-level ozone is a pollutant.</td></tr><tr><td>x Global warming = ozone depletion. x Different problems with different causes.</td></tr><tr><td>x CO2 is the only greenhouse gas. x CH4, N2O, CFCs are also significant.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Pollution of air, water and land has distinct sources, effects and control measures. Air pollutants are classified as primary, such as SO2, NOx, CO and particulates emitted directly, and secondary, such as ground-level ozone and acid rain, which form in the atmosphere. Water pollution from point and non-point sources causes organic pollution with a raised BOD, eutrophication and heavy-metal toxicity, while solid waste is managed by the 3Rs, landfilling, incineration and composting. At global scale, greenhouse gases drive the enhanced greenhouse effect, and ozone-depleting CFCs cause ozone-layer depletion, addressed by the Kyoto Protocol, the Paris Agreement and the Montreal Protocol respectively.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Air, water, and solid waste pollution</td><td>Pollution of air, water and land has distinct sources, effects and control measures. Air pollutants are classified as primary, such as SO2, NOx, CO and particulates emitted directly, and secondary, such as ground-level ozone and acid rain, which form in the atmosphere. Water pollution from point and non-point sources causes organic pollution with a raised BOD, eutrophication and heavy-metal toxicity, while solid waste is managed by the 3Rs, landfilling, incineration and composting. At global scale, greenhouse gases drive the enhanced greenhouse effect, and ozone-depleting CFCs cause ozone-layer depletion, addressed by the Kyoto Protocol, the Paris Agreement and the Montreal Protocol respectively.</td></tr></table></body></html>